1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:20,420
Back in New York, I am the head of development for a nonprofit called Robin Hood.

2
00:00:20,420 --> 00:00:24,660
When I'm not fighting poverty, I'm fighting fires as the assistant captain of a volunteer

3
00:00:24,660 --> 00:00:26,460
fire company.

4
00:00:26,460 --> 00:00:30,860
In our town, where the volunteers supplement a highly skilled career staff, you have to

5
00:00:30,860 --> 00:00:34,900
get to the fire scene pretty early to get in on any action.

6
00:00:34,900 --> 00:00:36,700
I remember my first fire.

7
00:00:36,700 --> 00:00:40,420
I was the second volunteer on the scene, so there was a pretty good chance I was going

8
00:00:40,420 --> 00:00:41,420
to get in.

9
00:00:41,420 --> 00:00:45,460
Still, it was a real foot race against the other volunteers to get to the captain in

10
00:00:45,460 --> 00:00:48,420
charge to find out what our assignments would be.

11
00:00:48,420 --> 00:00:53,620
When I found the captain, he was having a very engaging conversation with the homeowner,

12
00:00:53,620 --> 00:00:57,700
who was surely having one of the worst days of her life.

13
00:00:57,700 --> 00:00:59,840
Here it was the middle of the night.

14
00:00:59,840 --> 00:01:05,940
She was standing outside in the pouring rain, under an umbrella, in her pajamas, barefoot,

15
00:01:05,940 --> 00:01:08,620
while her house was in flames.

16
00:01:08,620 --> 00:01:16,220
The other volunteer who had arrived just before me, let's call him Lex Luthor, got to the

17
00:01:16,220 --> 00:01:25,380
fireman captain first, and was asked to go inside and save the homeowner's dog, the dog.

18
00:01:25,380 --> 00:01:27,260
I was stunned with jealousy.

19
00:01:27,260 --> 00:01:31,420
Here was some lawyer or money manager who, for the rest of his life, gets to tell people

20
00:01:31,420 --> 00:01:36,320
that he went into a burning building to save a living creature, just because he beat me

21
00:01:36,320 --> 00:01:37,820
by five seconds.

22
00:01:37,820 --> 00:01:40,260
Well, I was next.

23
00:01:40,260 --> 00:01:44,660
The captain weighed me over, said, Bezos, I need you to go into the house.

24
00:01:44,700 --> 00:01:50,500
I need you to go upstairs, past the fire, and I need you to get this woman a pair of shoes.

25
00:01:50,500 --> 00:01:55,660
I swear.

26
00:01:55,660 --> 00:02:00,860
So not exactly what I was hoping for, but off I went.

27
00:02:00,860 --> 00:02:04,500
Up the stairs, down the hall, past the real firefighters, who were pretty much done putting

28
00:02:04,500 --> 00:02:10,020
out the fire at this point, into the master bedroom to get a pair of shoes.

29
00:02:11,020 --> 00:02:19,860
I know what you're thinking, but I'm no hero.

30
00:02:19,860 --> 00:02:25,340
I carried my payload back downstairs where I met my nemesis and the precious dog by the

31
00:02:25,340 --> 00:02:27,340
front door.

32
00:02:27,340 --> 00:02:32,860
We took our treasures outside to the homeowner where, not surprisingly, his received much

33
00:02:32,860 --> 00:02:35,620
more attention than did mine.

34
00:02:35,620 --> 00:02:39,500
A few weeks later, the department received a letter from the homeowner thanking us for

35
00:02:39,500 --> 00:02:42,620
the valiant effort displayed in saving her home.

36
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:50,300
The act of kindness she noted above all others, someone had even gotten her a pair of shoes.

37
00:02:50,300 --> 00:02:54,820
You know, in both my vocation at Robin Hood and my avocation as volunteer firefighter,

38
00:02:54,820 --> 00:02:59,860
I am witness to acts of generosity and kindness on a monumental scale.

39
00:02:59,860 --> 00:03:03,420
But I'm also witness to acts of grace and courage on an individual basis.

40
00:03:03,420 --> 00:03:06,280
And you know what I've learned?

41
00:03:06,280 --> 00:03:08,220
They all matter.

42
00:03:08,220 --> 00:03:12,260
So as I look around this room at people who either have achieved or are on their way to

43
00:03:12,260 --> 00:03:18,320
achieving remarkable levels of success, I would offer this reminder.

44
00:03:18,320 --> 00:03:20,580
Don't wait.

45
00:03:20,580 --> 00:03:23,860
Don't wait until you make your first million to make a difference in somebody's life.

46
00:03:23,860 --> 00:03:27,660
If you have something to give, give it now.

47
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:32,940
Serve food at a soup kitchen, clean up a neighborhood park, be a mentor.

48
00:03:32,940 --> 00:03:38,100
Not every day is going to offer us a chance to save somebody's life, but every day offers

49
00:03:38,100 --> 00:03:40,400
us an opportunity to affect one.

50
00:03:40,400 --> 00:03:42,080
So get in the game.

51
00:03:42,080 --> 00:03:43,460
Save the shoes.

52
00:03:43,460 --> 00:03:44,460
Thank you.

53
00:03:44,460 --> 00:03:45,460
Come back.

54
00:03:45,460 --> 00:03:46,460
Come back.

55
00:03:46,460 --> 00:03:46,480
Thank you.

56
00:04:09,100 --> 00:04:13,540
Somewhere in America, there's a doctor who can peer into the future.

57
00:04:13,540 --> 00:04:18,180
There's a nurse who can access in an instant every patient's past.

58
00:04:18,180 --> 00:04:23,020
And because the whole hospital's working together, there's a family who can breathe easy right

59
00:04:23,020 --> 00:04:24,860
now.

60
00:04:24,860 --> 00:04:28,660
Somewhere in America, we've already answered some of the nation's toughest health care

61
00:04:28,660 --> 00:04:29,660
questions.

62
00:04:29,660 --> 00:04:35,660
And the over 60,000 people of Siemens are ready to do it again.

63
00:04:35,660 --> 00:04:37,540
Siemens answers.

64
00:04:38,100 --> 00:04:40,100
We'll be right back.

